<header class="page-head docs-head">
    <div>
        <h1>Workflow and integrations</h1>
        <p class="lede">
            The plumbing around the code: executing a batch of calls, keeping
            a plan, remembering project facts, and driving Git, GitHub and
            Docker without leaving the client.
        </p>
    </div>
</header>

<div class="docs-body prose">
    <h2><code>run_plan</code>: a graph of calls, one round trip</h2>
    <p>
        <code>run_plan</code> executes a whole dependency graph
        <strong>inside the server</strong>, with no model in the loop. A step
        is either a tool call — any tool, by name, with the argument names its
        schema uses — or a shell command. Steps declare what they
        <code>dependsOn</code> and which <code>paths</code> they write: two
        steps whose paths overlap never run at the same time, which is what
        makes parallel edits safe. The plan is validated before anything runs
        — a duplicate id, an unknown tool or a cycle rejects the whole thing —
        and a failed step skips its dependents.
    </p>
    <pre><code>{
  "name": "tidy-and-verify",
  "steps": [
    { "id": "simplify", "kind": "tool", "tool": "simplify_code",
      "arguments": { "scope": "project", "projectName": "MyApp",
                     "rules": "IDE0028,IDE0041", "dryRun": false },
      "paths": ["src/MyApp"] },
    { "id": "build", "kind": "command",
      "command": "dotnet build src/MyApp/MyApp.csproj -c Release",
      "dependsOn": ["simplify"], "timeoutSeconds": 300 }
  ]
}</code></pre>
    <p>
        Use it for <strong>mechanical</strong> work — the same operation over
        many files, or a build that depends on a set of edits. Steps that need
        judgement between them are still the model's job.
    </p>

    <h2>Planning and memory</h2>
    <p>
        The board lives in the solution store, so it survives restarts and is
        scoped to the solution: cards move across
        <code>backlog → todo → in_progress → review → done</code>, carry
        comments and full change history, and <code>board_brief</code>
        answers “what is missing, what comes next” in one compact call.
        <code>create_task_checklist</code> puts a multi-step operation on the
        board as one card with the steps as a checklist.
    </p>
    <p>
        Memory is the board's second lane: a fact, a decision or a constraint
        goes to <code>board_remember</code> under a topic — one current
        version per topic, writing again supersedes instead of duplicating —
        and <code>board_recall</code> reads it back. Pinned notes ride along
        in <code>board_brief</code> and in <code>read_project_docs</code>,
        which is what makes writing one worth the call.
    </p>

    <h2>Git, as data</h2>
    <p>
        The daily flow and the history operations that usually need a
        terminal: status, diff, log, blame, stage and commit, branches,
        squash, amend, reset, merge, rebase, cherry-pick, revert, stash, named
        patches, remotes, tags, push and pull. The interesting part is what
        stops being terminal-shaped:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>A merge conflict is data.</strong> <code>git_conflicts</code> lists the conflicted files with their hunks — ours, theirs, base — and resolves each hunk by strategy or custom content, then stages the file and tells you whether to continue or abort.</li>
        <li><strong>An interactive rebase without an editor.</strong> <code>git_rebase action=plan</code> takes a JSON list of pick/reword/squash/fixup/drop steps and executes it exactly; <code>reword</code> and <code>squash</code> take their message from the step.</li>
        <li><strong>Destructive actions are gated.</strong> <code>git_reset</code> with <code>mode=hard</code> and <code>git_sync</code> with <code>force=true</code> require <code>confirm=true</code>; force uses <code>--force-with-lease</code>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>GitHub and other hosts</h2>
    <p>
        Repositories, pull requests (with line-anchored reviews), issues,
        releases, Actions runs and secrets, branch protection — eight tools
        over the authenticated GitHub CLI, with destructive actions gated
        behind <code>confirm=true</code>. GitLab, Azure DevOps and Bitbucket
        Cloud are served by <code>git_host</code>, which reads the provider
        and project from the git remote. Before reviewing anything,
        <code>code_review</code> returns the brief a reviewer starts from:
        changed files, the signals that matter first, the worst methods of
        each changed file, and the findings that land in them.
    </p>

    <h2>Docker</h2>
    <p>
        The container lifecycle: status, inventory (containers, images,
        networks, volumes), inspect, logs, stats, run and control, compose
        up/down/logs/restart, and a prune that requires
        <code>confirm=true</code> because it is irreversible. Structured
        results instead of <code>docker ps</code> output the agent has to
        parse again.
    </p>

    <h2>Files and the three searches</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong><code>search_text</code></strong> is grep with structure: regex, glob filter, context, <code>file:line</code>. For configs, logs, headers and arbitrary text.</li>
        <li><strong><code>text_index</code></strong> is a full-text index over the whole solution: words are matched inside identifiers, results are BM25-ranked — for “where is this named or documented?”.</li>
        <li><strong><code>reference_index</code></strong> is the symbol index: declarations and typed usages — call, read, write, inherit, implement, override — plus callers, callees, hierarchy and outline.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        Around them: <code>read_files</code> for several known paths in one
        budgeted call, <code>read_tree</code> for the shape of a folder,
        <code>move_path</code> and <code>download_file</code>.
    </p>

    <h2>HTTP and SignalR</h2>
    <p>
        <code>http_invoke</code> calls a REST endpoint of a running app — any
        method, headers, JSON body — and the <code>signal_r_*</code> family
        connects to any ASP.NET Core SignalR hub, subscribes to a method,
        buffers the messages and invokes hub methods. This is how an agent
        tests a live API or a hub without writing a client:
        <code>run_project</code> → read the listening URL →
        <code>http_invoke</code> / <code>signal_r_connect</code>.
    </p>

    <h2>Audit and undo</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><code>command_log</code> — one row per tool call, with the arguments (credential-looking values redacted).</li>
        <li><code>edit_history</code> — one row per file this server wrote, with the tool, the rules and whether it is still revertible.</li>
        <li><code>revert_edit</code> — restore the file to its state before one recorded edit, refusing if the file changed after it.</li>
        <li><code>local_history</code> — whole-file snapshots independent of commits: capture, walk, diff, restore.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        Everything the server does to your solution is a row you can read —
        and, for writes, a state you can put back.
    </p>
</div>

